0..70 Comment "// Corten\'s running example as THIR written by hand, its postcondition"
71..140 Comment "// derived in a weakest-precondition calculus (Farka et al., \"Corten:"
141..218 Comment "// Foundational Verification of Rust Programs\", arXiv:2609.04372, 2026, § 4,"
219..293 Comment "// Fig. 2): `example` returns early from inside `identity`\'s argument. The"
294..361 Comment "// calculus is the specification monad of THIR\'s control effect: an"
362..436 Comment "// expression\'s precondition takes one postcondition for the value it ends"
437..510 Comment "// at and one for a `return`, a `return` sends its operand\'s value to the"
511..585 Comment "// second, and a function\'s guard, the effect\'s handler, hands both to the"
586..660 Comment "// caller\'s continuation (§ 4.2, Defs. 4.1, 4.2 and 4.10). Departing from"
661..733 Comment "// § 4: `break`, `continue` and `nomatch` are left out, `u8` is read as"
734..808 Comment "// `Integer`, each function takes one parameter, and propositions are read"
809..883 Comment "// as types. The axioms are written twice. As the rules of `Thir.Wp`, over"
884..956 Comment "// THIR\'s nodes as its operations, the checker orients them, joins their"
957..1031 Comment "// pairs in the second-order reading, and fires them at an inspected call:"
1032..1107 Comment "// `example`\'s guarded body at false ends at its return continuation, never"
1108..1181 Comment "// reaching `identity`. As a fold of THIR\'s data, the normaliser computes"
1182..1252 Comment "// each precondition and the kernel replays it, so `example` meets its"
1253..1322 Comment "// specification at every value and postcondition given `identity`\'s."
1324..1383 Comment "/// A value the fragment computes: an integer or a boolean."
